            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Questions · Biology</p>
    <h1>What Do Raven Eggs Look Like?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Size, colour, clutch size, and nesting biology</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Raven eggs are <strong>pale blue-green to greenish-grey, spotted and blotched with brown and grey</strong>. They measure approximately 48–55 mm × 32–38 mm — roughly the size of a large chicken egg. Clutch size is 3–7 eggs (typically 4–6). The female incubates alone for 20–21 days; the male feeds her on the nest.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Egg Appearance</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) eggs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ground colour:</strong> Pale blue-green, greenish-grey, or occasionally pale green</li>
      <li><strong>Markings:</strong> Heavily spotted and blotched with brown, olive-brown, and grey — spots variable in size, often concentrated at the broader end</li>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> 48–55 mm long × 32–38 mm wide (mean approximately 50 × 33 mm)</li>
      <li><strong>Shape:</strong> Ovate to slightly elongated</li>
      <li><strong>Texture:</strong> Smooth, slightly glossy</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Egg colouration is variable; some clutches are more heavily marked than others. The spotted pattern is typical of open-cup nesters and may provide camouflage if the nest is partially exposed.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Clutch Size and Incubation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clutch size:</strong> 3–7 eggs; 4–6 most common</li>
      <li><strong>Incubation period:</strong> 20–21 days</li>
      <li><strong>Who incubates:</strong> Female only; male brings food to the incubating female</li>
      <li><strong>Broods per year:</strong> 1 (rarely 2 in favourable conditions)</li>
      <li><strong>Fledging:</strong> Chicks leave the nest at approximately 35–42 days</li>
      <li><strong>Post-fledging dependence:</strong> Young ravens remain with parents for several months after fledging</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Nest</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens typically build large, bulky nests:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Structure:</strong> Large platform of sticks and twigs; lined with wool, hair, bark fibre, grass, and moss</li>
      <li><strong>Location:</strong> Cliff ledges (preferred), tall trees, pylons, buildings — high, inaccessible sites</li>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> Can be 60–120 cm across after years of reuse; ravens often reuse and add to the same nest annually</li>
      <li><strong>Breeding season:</strong> Eggs laid February–May in the northern hemisphere (varies by latitude); earlier in southern parts of range</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both sexes build the nest, though the female does more of the lining. Nest sites may be used for many years — some sites are known to have been occupied for decades.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Comparison with Similar Species</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> For identification context:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> 48–55 mm; pale blue-green, heavily spotted</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion Crow (<em>Corvus corone</em>):</strong> 44–46 mm; similar pale green-blue with brown spots — difficult to distinguish without exact measurements</li>
      <li><strong>Rook (<em>Corvus frugilegus</em>):</strong> 40–44 mm; paler, less heavily spotted</li>
      <li><strong>Jackdaw (<em>Corvus monedula</em>):</strong> 34–36 mm; white to pale blue with brown spots; significantly smaller</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In practice, the nest location (cliff vs treetop vs cavity) is a more reliable identification feature than egg colouration alone.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="id-sources id-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="id-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Madge, S. &amp; Burn, H. (1994). <em>Crows and Jays</em>. Christopher Helm. — egg descriptions, clutch size, incubation data.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Boarman, W. I. &amp; Heinrich, B. (1999). Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). In A. Poole &amp; F. Gill (Eds.), <em>The Birds of North America</em>, No. 476. — breeding biology.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="id-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> field-guide and species-account data</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="id-nav-bottom">
    <a href="/questions/do-ravens-mate-for-life/" class="id-back">← Do Ravens Mate for Life?</a>
    <a href="/species/common-raven/" class="id-next">Common Raven Overview →</a>
  </nav>
</article>
